WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.220
An inverse function reverses an input-output pairing

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.443
This line sends input one to output five

00:00:06.443 --> 00:00:14.500
If it has an inverse, that inverse must send five back to one, undoing the operations in the reverse order.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.411
To undo the rule, first subtract three from the output, then divide by two

00:00:21.411 --> 00:00:25.820
Solving the original equation for its input gives this reverse instruction

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:32.233
Renaming the new input variable leads to inverse of x equals x minus three over two.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:38.722
Swapping input and output reflects the graph across the line y equals x

00:00:38.722 --> 00:00:41.516
The point one, five becomes five, one

00:00:41.516 --> 00:00:49.100
The inverse symbol means reversing a function; it does not mean the reciprocal one divided by the function value.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.956
A function needs unique reverse outputs to have an inverse

00:00:54.956 --> 00:01:00.878
Squaring over all real numbers fails because positive and negative inputs share outputs

00:01:00.878 --> 00:01:08.167
Restricting its input to nonnegative values makes the reverse unambiguous and gives the principal square-root function.
